---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Couch to 5K on a Treadmill (2026): The Nine-Week Plan";
const description =
  "The full nine-week walk-to-run progression adapted for a treadmill, with speeds to set, what to do when a week is too hard, and the deck length you actually need.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Couch to 5K on a Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/couch-to-5k-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What speed should I run for Couch to 5K on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most beginners should run the intervals at 4.5 to 5.5 mph — a 12 to 13-minute mile — and walk at 3 to 3.5 mph. That running pace feels slow and is correct: the plan's difficulty is meant to come from duration, not speed. You should be able to speak in short phrases during a running interval.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you do Couch to 5K entirely on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it suits the plan well. The pace is held for you, which prevents the most common beginner error of running the intervals too fast; the intervals are exactly timed; and weather and daylight stop mattering across nine weeks. The main adaptation is allowing for the few seconds the belt takes to change speed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What if I cannot finish a week of Couch to 5K?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Repeat it, and try it 0.5 mph slower first — most \"I cannot do week five\" problems are pace problems rather than fitness problems. Taking twelve or fourteen weeks to complete a nine-week plan is a success. The only reason to stop rather than adjust is sharp or localised pain, or pain that persists into the next day.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I use incline during Couch to 5K?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Set 0 to 1% and leave it. This programme builds the ability to run continuously, and adding grade adds a second stimulus while you are still adapting to the first. Save incline work for after week nine, when the habit and the base are established.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a 60-inch deck for Couch to 5K?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not for this plan. At 4.5 to 5.5 mph a 55-inch deck is adequate for most people through all nine weeks. It starts to matter if you keep running and get faster — above about 6.5 mph a 55-inch deck constrains anyone much over 5'8\", and you shorten your stride to stay clear of the rear roller.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do my shins hurt during Couch to 5K?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually too much too soon, or running the intervals too fast — both very common in the first three weeks. Slow the running pace, make rest days genuinely restful, and check your shoes, since a midsole with several hundred miles on it has lost most of its compression. Sharp or persistent pain means stop and get advice.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-08",
      dateModified: "2026-09-08",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-08",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-08",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "couch to 5k treadmill",
      "c25k",
      "beginner running",
      "treadmill running plan",
      "learn to run",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Plan", link: "#the-plan" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-couchto5ktreadmill" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-couchto5ktreadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Training Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Couch to 5K <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">on a Treadmill</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Nine weeks, three sessions a week, from walking to thirty minutes of continuous running. A treadmill makes the pacing easier and introduces two problems the plan does not warn you about.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#the-plan" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Nine-Week Plan
          </a>
          <a href="#setting-speeds" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Setting Your Speeds
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 8, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">Before you start.</strong> We review treadmills rather than
          coach runners. If you have a heart condition, high blood pressure, joint problems, are
          pregnant, are significantly overweight, or have been inactive for a long time, speak to
          your doctor before beginning a running programme. Stop for chest pain, unusual
          breathlessness, dizziness or an irregular heartbeat. Sharp joint pain means stop that
          session, not push through it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-treadmill" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Why a Treadmill Suits This Plan</a>
          <a href="#the-plan" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Nine-Week Plan</a>
          <a href="#setting-speeds" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Setting Your Speeds</a>
          <a href="#treadmill-traps" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Two Treadmill-Specific Traps</a>
          <a href="#deck-length" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. The Deck Length You Actually Need</a>
          <a href="#when-a-week-is-too-hard" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. When a Week Is Too Hard</a>
          <a href="#common-problems" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Problems That Come Up, and What They Usually Mean</a>
          <a href="#form" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. A Few Words on Form</a>
          <a href="#after-week-nine" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. After Week Nine</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. The Short Version</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The walk-to-run progression works because it treats running as something to be built
          rather than attempted. A treadmill suits it unusually well — the pace is held for you,
          the surface is forgiving, and the weather is irrelevant — provided you avoid two
          traps that outdoor runners never encounter.
        </p>

        <div id="why-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why a Treadmill Suits This Plan</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The pace is held for you.</strong> The single most common beginner error is
            running the running intervals too fast, then finding week four impossible. A treadmill
            removes the decision — you set 5 mph and 5 mph is what happens, regardless of how
            enthusiastic you feel in minute two.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The intervals are exact.</strong> A plan built on 60-second runs and 90-second
            walks is easier to follow when a console is counting, and there is no temptation to
            stretch or shorten a rep by feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The surface is consistent and cushioned.</strong> No kerbs, no camber, no
            uneven paving, and a deck that is more forgiving than tarmac — which matters in the
            first weeks when your legs are least prepared.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weather and daylight stop mattering.</strong> Nine weeks is long enough that a
            spell of bad weather or dark evenings would otherwise break the streak, and the
            programme depends on the streak.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Nobody is watching.</strong> A meaningful barrier for a lot of new runners,
            and one a home treadmill removes entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Nine-Week Plan</h2>
<p>
            This is the widely used nine-week structure: three sessions a week, on non-consecutive
            days, each preceded by a five-minute brisk walk to warm up and followed by a
            five-minute walk to cool down. Those ten minutes are part of the session.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
            <table class="min-w-[540px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Week</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Each session (after the warm-up walk)</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Eight rounds of: 60 seconds running, 90 seconds walking</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">2</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Six rounds of: 90 seconds running, 2 minutes walking</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Two rounds of: 90 seconds running, 90 seconds walking, 3 minutes running, 3 minutes walking</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">4</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3 min run, 90 sec walk, 5 min run, 2.5 min walk, 3 min run, 90 sec walk, 5 min run</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Day 1: 5 min run × 3 with 3 min walks. Day 2: 8 min run × 2 with a 5 min walk. Day 3: 20 minutes continuous</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">6</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Day 1: 5 min, 3 min walk, 8 min, 3 min walk, 5 min. Day 2: 10 min × 2 with a 3 min walk. Day 3: 25 minutes continuous</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">25 minutes continuous, three times</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">8</td><td class="px-4 py-2">28 minutes continuous, three times</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold align-top">9</td><td class="px-4 py-2">30 minutes continuous, three times</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Note that week five is the first where the three sessions differ, and the jump to
            twenty continuous minutes on day three is the point most people find hardest. It is
            also, reliably, achievable — the previous four weeks did the work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setting-speeds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Setting Your Speeds</h2>
<p>
            Two speeds cover the whole nine weeks, and the running one should be slower than you
            expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking intervals: 3 to 3.5 mph.</strong> Brisk enough to be recovery rather
            than rest. If you finish the walk feeling you needed longer, slow it to 3 mph rather
            than shortening the running interval.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running intervals: 4.5 to 5.5 mph for most beginners.</strong> That is a 12 to
            13-minute mile, which feels almost comically slow and is correct. The plan's difficulty
            comes from duration, not pace, and running the intervals fast is the single most
            common reason people fail at week four or five.
          </p>
          <p>
            The calibration that matters: during a running interval you should be able to speak in
            short phrases. If you can only manage single words, you are too fast — slow down even
            if the number offends you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Set 0 to 1% incline</strong> and leave it. This is not the programme for
            incline work; add nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you think in minutes per mile rather than mph, our
            <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a>
            converts between them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treadmill-traps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Two Treadmill-Specific Traps</h2>
<p>
            <strong>One: the belt does not slow down when you tire.</strong> Outdoors you
            decelerate naturally as an interval gets hard. On a belt the speed continues until you
            press something, which means an interval set slightly too fast becomes progressively
            more punishing rather than self-correcting. Set the running speed for how you will
            feel in the last interval, not the first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two: changing speed takes several seconds.</strong> Most machines take three
            to six seconds to move between walking and running pace. On a 60-second interval that
            is a meaningful share of the rep spent in transition. Press the button a couple of
            seconds before the interval is due to start, and accept that the first and last few
            seconds of each rep are a ramp.
          </p>
          <p>
            A practical solution for the early weeks: set the running speed and use the machine's
            quick-select buttons rather than incrementing by 0.1, so the change is one press. Some
            machines also let you programme intervals directly, which removes the problem entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Never step onto a moving belt</strong> to start a running interval. Change the
            speed while walking on it, or straddle the deck on the side rails and step on once it
            is at pace. And wear the safety key.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Deck Length You Actually Need</h2>
<p>
            This is an equipment site, so one honest note about the machine. At the speeds this
            plan uses — 4.5 to 5.5 mph — deck length is much less critical than it is for faster
            running, and a 55-inch deck is genuinely adequate for most people through all nine
            weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where it starts to matter is if you continue past week nine and get faster. Stride
            length rises sharply with pace, and above about 6.5 mph a 55-inch deck begins to
            constrain anyone much over 5'8" — you shorten your stride to stay clear of the rear
            roller, which changes your gait and is a common source of calf complaints.
          </p>
          <p>
            So: do not let deck length stop you starting. If you already own a treadmill, it is
            almost certainly sufficient for this plan. If you are buying specifically to do this
            and you suspect you will keep running afterwards, a 60-inch deck is the
            specification worth having, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running treadmill guide</a>
            covers it. If you are over six feet, that guidance is firmer — see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide for tall runners</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-a-week-is-too-hard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. When a Week Is Too Hard</h2>
<p>
            Repeating a week is not failure, and it is the most useful adjustment in the whole
            programme. Plenty of people take twelve or fourteen weeks to complete a nine-week plan,
            and they finish it — which is the only outcome that matters.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Repeat the week</strong> if you could not complete the sessions, or completed
            them only by stopping early. Do the same week again in full before moving on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Slow down before you repeat.</strong> If the intervals were completed but
            brutal, try the same week 0.5 mph slower first. Most "I cannot do week five" problems
            are pace problems rather than fitness problems.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Split the difference</strong> if a jump feels too big — week five day three's
            twenty continuous minutes is the usual sticking point. Running fifteen minutes,
            walking two, then running five more is a legitimate intermediate step.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Take an extra rest day</strong> if you are sore rather than tired. The plan
            specifies non-consecutive days for a reason, and four days between sessions is better
            than an injury.
          </p>
          <p>
            The only reason to stop rather than adjust is pain that is sharp, localised, or
            persists into the following day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="common-problems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Problems That Come Up, and What They Usually Mean</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Shin soreness.</strong> Extremely common in the first three weeks, usually
            from doing too much too soon or from running the intervals too fast. Slow the running
            pace, ensure rest days are genuinely rest, and check that your shoes are not several
            years old — a midsole with 600 miles on it is a much harder surface than it was.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Breathlessness that arrives immediately.</strong> Almost always pace. Beginners
            routinely run 1 to 1.5 mph faster than they should. Drop the speed until you can manage
            short phrases.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A stitch.</strong> Slow to a walk, breathe deeply and evenly, and it usually
            passes within a minute. Eating within an hour of a session makes it more likely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Feeling fine during, wrecked afterwards.</strong> Usually a hydration or fuel
            issue, or simply too much too soon. Check the cool-down is actually happening — five
            walking minutes at the end matters more than people expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Boredom.</strong> A legitimate problem, and the most common reason treadmill
            plans stop. Something with a plot works better than music for the longer weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="form" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. A Few Words on Form</h2>
<p>
            Beginner runners are told a great deal about form, most of it unnecessary. Your body
            will find a reasonable pattern on its own, and over-thinking it usually makes things
            worse rather than better. Three things are worth attention.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not hold the handrails while running.</strong> It alters your gait, removes
            arm drive, and makes the effort dishonest. If you feel you need to hold on, the speed
            is too high.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Run in the middle of the belt.</strong> New treadmill runners drift toward the
            front, close to the console, because it feels safer. It means every stride ends near
            the deck's front edge and you are constantly making small corrections. Aim for the
            centre.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Relax your shoulders and hands.</strong> Tension travels, and clenched fists
            and raised shoulders make a run feel harder than it is. Check them periodically and
            let them drop.
          </p>
          <p>
            Everything else — cadence, foot strike, arm angle — is a refinement for later, and
            not something to think about while learning to run at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-week-nine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. After Week Nine</h2>
<p>
            Thirty continuous minutes at 5 mph is about 2.5 miles, so most people finish the plan
            able to run for the duration of a 5K without quite covering the distance. Closing that
            gap takes two or three more weeks of simply extending the run.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The first priority is maintaining the habit</strong>, not improving. Three
            thirty-minute runs a week, held for a month, consolidates everything the nine weeks
            built. Resist adding speed immediately.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Then add duration to one session</strong> — take one run to 35 and then 40
            minutes while keeping the other two at 30. That is the standard way to build endurance
            and it is how you reach the full 5K distance comfortably.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Add intervals last</strong>, and only one session a week. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            has beginner structures, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">training guide</a>
            covers how to build a balanced week once you are past the plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you would rather stay at walking intensity, incline walking is an excellent
            alternative destination — the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 protocol</a>
            is the best-known version and demands nothing above a walking pace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. The Short Version</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Three sessions a week on non-consecutive days</strong>, five minutes of walking
            either side of every one, for nine weeks. Walking intervals at 3 to 3.5 mph, running
            intervals at 4.5 to 5.5 mph.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Run the intervals slower than you want to.</strong> Almost every failure at
            week four or five is a pace problem, and the plan's difficulty is meant to come from
            duration.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Repeat any week you could not complete</strong>, and try it 0.5 mph slower
            before repeating. Taking twelve weeks to finish a nine-week plan is a success.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set the incline to 0 or 1% and leave it alone. This is not the programme for hills.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What speed should I run for Couch to 5K on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most beginners should run the intervals at 4.5 to 5.5 mph — a 12 to 13-minute mile — and walk at 3 to 3.5 mph. That running pace feels slow and is correct: the plan's difficulty is meant to come from duration, not speed. You should be able to speak in short phrases during a running interval.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you do Couch to 5K entirely on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it suits the plan well. The pace is held for you, which prevents the most common beginner error of running the intervals too fast; the intervals are exactly timed; and weather and daylight stop mattering across nine weeks. The main adaptation is allowing for the few seconds the belt takes to change speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What if I cannot finish a week of Couch to 5K?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Repeat it, and try it 0.5 mph slower first — most "I cannot do week five" problems are pace problems rather than fitness problems. Taking twelve or fourteen weeks to complete a nine-week plan is a success. The only reason to stop rather than adjust is sharp or localised pain, or pain that persists into the next day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use incline during Couch to 5K?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Set 0 to 1% and leave it. This programme builds the ability to run continuously, and adding grade adds a second stimulus while you are still adapting to the first. Save incline work for after week nine, when the habit and the base are established.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 60-inch deck for Couch to 5K?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for this plan. At 4.5 to 5.5 mph a 55-inch deck is adequate for most people through all nine weeks. It starts to matter if you keep running and get faster — above about 6.5 mph a 55-inch deck constrains anyone much over 5'8", and you shorten your stride to stay clear of the rear roller.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do my shins hurt during Couch to 5K?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually too much too soon, or running the intervals too fast — both very common in the first three weeks. Slow the running pace, make rest days genuinely restful, and check your shoes, since a midsole with several hundred miles on it has lost most of its compression. Sharp or persistent pain means stop and get advice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes on Couch to 5K</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first accounts for most of the failures at weeks four and five, and it is entirely avoidable.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the intervals too fast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Beginners routinely run 1 to 1.5 mph quicker than they should, which is survivable in week one and impossible by week five. The plan is built so the difficulty comes from duration. If you cannot speak in short phrases during a running interval, slow down — even if the number on the console feels embarrassing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a repeated week as failure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Repeating is the most useful adjustment in the programme, and plenty of people take twelve or fourteen weeks to complete it. Finishing is the only outcome that matters. Try the week again 0.5 mph slower before repeating it at the same pace — the problem is usually speed rather than fitness.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the walking warm-up and cool-down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The five minutes either side are part of the session, not optional padding. Cold legs into a running interval is the most avoidable injury pattern there is, and stepping straight off a belt after effort produces a disorientation that catches people out. Build both into the time you allocate.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running on consecutive days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The plan specifies non-consecutive days because adaptation happens during recovery, and new runners are adapting connective tissue as well as fitness. Three sessions across a week with rest between them beats three in four days. If you are sore rather than tired, take an extra day.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

